aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186157745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155278 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186157745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302015 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186157745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186157745 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186157745 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186157745 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302426 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302428 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302083 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166952 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302157 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166953 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302161 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302324 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186166976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302342 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163998 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301235 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167163 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167163 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n2. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n3. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167163 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164004 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301770 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167164 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167164 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = πx_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167164 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164034 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301434 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167169 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3(2s-5)}{s(s-3)}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167169 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) =$ {{field-1}},\n$X_{2}(s) = (3(2s - 5))/(s(s - 3))$." 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167169 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}-2L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)}+2L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-9={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-6=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(s-1){X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)=9 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-2){X}_{2}(s)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=6-(s-2){X}_{2}(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1)[6-(s-2){X}_{2}(s)]+2{X}_{2}(s) & =9 \\\\ 6(s-1)-[(s-1)(s-2)-2]{X}_{2}(s) & =9 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-3s){X}_{2}(s) & =9-6(s-1) \\\\ -s(s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =-6s+15 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3(2s-5)}{s(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =6-(s-2){X}_{2}(s) \\\\  & =6-(s-2)\\cdot \\frac{3(2s-5)}{s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6s(s-3)-3(s-2)(2s-5)}{s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3s-10)}{s(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3(3s-10)}{s(s-3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3(2s-5)}{s(s-3)}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164038 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301562 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167171 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167171 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x^{2} - y^{2}, y(0) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167171 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1+(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{16}$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164045 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301512 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167173 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186167173 1071 "$y^{‴} + y^{′} = \\sec x, {y_{1} = 1, y_{2} = \\cos (x), y_{3} = \\sin (x)}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B\\cos (x) + C\\sin (x) + u_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)\\cos (x) + u_{3}(x)\\sin (x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_{3}(x)$?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186167173 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\