na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221929 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t) + 7x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t ∈ R$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The function $a(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}7e^{-4t} \\\\ 2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-1}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $b(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ -2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-2}}\na solution of the system.\n\nThe function $c(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n{{field-3}}\na solution of the system." 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221929 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(7{e}^{-4t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{-4t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+7\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2\\cdot 7{e}^{-4t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-28{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (-2{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot (-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot (-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-20{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {e}^{t}+7\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\ -2\\cdot {e}^{t}+3\\cdot {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,c}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(7{e}^{-4t})\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220162 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220163 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301448 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301492 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221931 1071 "Find $e^{At}$ for $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $1$.*\n\n| $e^{At} = \\frac{1}{5}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-7e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221931 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}$ |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221931 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $4$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-6{e}^{-4t}+6{e}^{t}))+({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220164 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301578 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221932 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}-3xy,\\,y(-2)=3\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ \n