" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186221933 1071 "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186221933 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{3!}{{s}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3!}{(s-4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227546 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186229533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301723 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229533 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.25,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current value $y?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229533 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x^{2} - y^{2}, y(0) = 1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx = 0.25$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **general form** of $g(y_{new})$ in terms of the new values $x_{new}$ and $y_{new}$, and the current value $y$?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186229533 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith $