9533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186229534 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186227558 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186229535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301551 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229535 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186229535 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x^{2} - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533413 17592186045585 false] [17592186229535 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299939 1081 "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299939 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299939 1084 #uuid "006c6611-e018-504a-a01c-32aef354674d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299940 1081 "1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299940 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299940 1084 #uuid "011fae4e-c40e-5543-bf07-c0c1656584a7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299941 1081 "1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299941 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299941 1084 #uuid "01c168f8-05c3-5f43-ac97-03fa4301d749" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299942 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186299942 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533413