13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302046 1069 "q-321292" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302046 1071 "Find the **exact solutions in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302046 1072 #uuid "797b32d4-d954-5e57-a08c-8b0e6c77d258" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302046 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain its not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301779 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1069 "q-321299" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1071 "![](images/6f/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1072 #uuid "17bceb35-b5fb-536b-bf3f-edb50187b769" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302047 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301601 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301675 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1069 "q-321306" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1071 "Express $z=14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1072 #uuid "309f8df2-ff1b-5330-aaa7-587c18b468d0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302048 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =14(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{3}-7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301827 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301905 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1069 "q-321307" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1071 "Express $z=16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1072 #uuid "24514d85-9074-586d-bd86-5782009ad2cb" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302049 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =8-8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301498 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301838 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1069 "q-321308" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1071 "Express $z=18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1072 #uuid "cabafb25-d8ad-503c-847c-e869adfd1bcc" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302050 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =18(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{18\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-9+9\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301379 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301943 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1069 "q-321309" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1071 "Express $z=20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1072 #uuid "d5d42d22-afab-5657-a00e-582139185cce" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302051 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =20(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-10\\sqrt{2}+10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301754 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301865 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1069 "q-321310" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1071 "Express $z=4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1072 #uuid "38098932-4e7b-5400-bb4a-1a39003f645b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302052 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =6+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301977 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1069 "q-321311" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1071 "Express $z=7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1072 #uuid "c913b645-6400-5a0c-a995-145873544dfe" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302053 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =7(0+\\text{i}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0+7\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301936 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1069 "q-321314" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1072 #uuid "746bed7b-e090-56a7-9ad1-4c0bfac39908" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302054 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301400 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1069 "q-321318" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1072 #uuid "3eac432e-a9f4-5e40-ae91-1f28e72debdf" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302055 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301804 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1069 "q-321322" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1071 "![](images/c9/c922feb998c41aae1ab40e3e6f240b07756699d4756b91458ff8271cec152f7f.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the ray shown above. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nThe polar equation is $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1072 #uuid "2909dc49-2e10-5471-88fa-907838be39eb" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302056 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301435 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1069 "q-32133" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1072 #uuid "f41cbfea-5742-50ae-9cb7-b244cecfac9e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302057 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301975 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1069 "q-321341" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1071 "![](images/67/67f1bac61a5f2c1e7c3e92c33fb556a43ec3a04969cf24abcf8d80639afb31e2.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the ray shown above. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nThe polar equation is $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1072 #uuid "6bde1879-6bc3-518a-aada-86b0995cd229" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302058 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThus, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301278 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302006 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1069 "q-321345" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1071 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1072 #uuid "55d3ab74-717a-5e4d-aa0b-4d52044b6f36" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302059 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{x}_{1} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=60\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=240\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301251 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301781 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1069 "q-321347" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1071 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1072 #uuid "2d054a95-51bc-5cb9-9dbe-815d03306268" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302060 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={180}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={150}^{∘}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={330}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=150\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=330\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301671 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1069 "q-32135" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-5 \\\\ 2x+3y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1072 #uuid "1aea68ff-444c-5905-8fef-602bfd366d39" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302061 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301417 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1069 "q-32137" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7x-y=0 \\\\ x+4y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1072 #uuid "74d2a5eb-cf80-54ce-b673-bcae2dc58e20" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302062 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{29}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301674 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1069 "q-32138" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=17 \\\\ x+4y=-17\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1072 #uuid "f7f53f6e-1e13-5cf9-98cc-1a6c8cbbaf2c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302063 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301370 13194139533413 17592186045585 