me{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301895 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1069 "q-321384" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1071 "![](images/af/afa5a80d73d9d8511cc35804277ad9dc3f1b62b2e1dbe3911d6ea9d665c3baa8.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ {{field-1}} \n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1072 #uuid "e1fe2936-ce2d-5b95-a874-57358178bd10" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302065 1074 "The angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, the polar equation is  \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301300 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301760 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301981 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1069 "q-321385" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (cos$\n$(${{field-2}}$)$\n$+\\,isin$\n$(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1072 #uuid "a5dceea2-37ec-5d79-9349-768d5f27de4e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302066 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $5-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(5-2\\text{i})}{(5+2\\text{i})(5-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{45}{29})}^{2}+{(\\frac{18}{29})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2025+324}{{29}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2349}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{45}{29}$ and $y=-\\frac{18}{29},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{18}{45}) \\\\  & ≈5.90\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.90)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.90)).\n$$