6b94a700-bd85-58aa-bc0c-7585b2834748" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302068 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2-x}{3})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301539 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301691 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302005 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1069 "q-321420" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1072 #uuid "89a81dab-08e9-5bc4-b7bc-65bffdc14b31" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302069 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1+\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301474 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301522 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301639 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1069 "q-321424" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1072 #uuid "384bec95-fb53-5352-981d-5e071dfd5a84" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302070 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}>0$ and $y=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301282 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301440 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301811 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1069 "q-321437" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1071 "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=8 \\\\ 4y=5+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  | |  |  |  | |  | $8$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $5$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $8$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $5$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1072 #uuid "51eb37cd-4f75-568b-a384-cdd97a21011c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302071 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y=8 \\\\ -3x+4y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301414 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301428 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301688 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1069 "q-321438" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1071 "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7y=-14 \\\\ 2y=5x+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | $2$ | $-7$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  | |  |  |  | |  | $-14$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2$ | $-7$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $-14$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1072 #uuid "2cc52558-5774-5127-8008-8b3f9f98aa60" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302072 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301390 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301630 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301917 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1069 "q-321439" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1071 "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x=3+y \\\\ 5y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  | |  |  |  | |  | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $10$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $10$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1072 #uuid "652b355c-c82f-51a3-ab10-8d285623bc25" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302073 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=3 \\\\ 0x+5y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301697 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1069 "q-32144" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1072 #uuid "90efc42e-c582-5ebf-b710-fc84d57b2391" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302074 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301261 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301747 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301960 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1069 "q-321441" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1071 "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y=4 \\\\ 5x-y=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | $-1$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  | |  |  |  | |  | $4$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-6$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-1$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $4$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $-6$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1072 #uuid "7cadcb98-fccb-55e1-8508-c8f471d2e367" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302075 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301785 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1069 "q-32146" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1072 #uuid "ea5fb524-6603-5687-8a02-9759396f0c0e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302076 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301556 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1069 "q-32148" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1072 #uuid "4d615245-8860-57bd-9182-e8271d3465f0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302077 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301460 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301907 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1069 "q-321592" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1072 #uuid "df968d14-24c9-53a0-ac49-2683ed9a8cca" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302078 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301676 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301683 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1069 "q-321613" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1072 #uuid "328224d2-452f-5f0c-ba2d-99488ec4490a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302079 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{2})=0.982\\,793…≈1.0\n$$\n\nSince $1.0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.982\\,793…≈4.1.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.0$ and $x=4.1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301360 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301840 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1069 "q-321622" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1071 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1072 #uuid "a823b8d4-ba47-5b33-ab00-0143319290ed" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302080 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{6})=9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${9}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={9}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{189}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={9}^{∘}$ and $x={189}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301373 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301715 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1069 "q-321633" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-7=0.5$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1072 #uuid "918ec36c-18d2-5442-8231-ebbc2c19880f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302081 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-7 & =0.5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(7.5)=1.438\\,244…≈1.4\n$$\n\nSince $1.4$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.4.$\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +1.438\\,244…≈4.6.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.4$ and $x=4.6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301456 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1069 "q-321645" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1072 #uuid "94f4999f-cddb-56aa-8dc1-690a88c2e2d4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302082 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301262 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1069 "q-321646" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1072 #uuid "775e3d7f-2959-5adc-b3eb-ee2a98bbde96" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302083 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301541 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301822 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1069 "q-321725" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1072 #uuid "c8c3245e-cb7f-5699-9644-faf5634111e4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302084 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=0.643\\,501…≈0.6\n$$\n\nSince $0.6$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.6.$\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.643\\,501…≈3.8.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.6$ and $x=3.8.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301368 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301585 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1069 "q-321728" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1071 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1072 #uuid "f383557e-242d-53fe-b746-7b0f1287a69b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302085 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.4)=-21.8014{…}^{∘}≈-{22.80}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{22.80}^{∘}$ lies outside the domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{158.20}^{∘},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={360}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{338.20}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={158}^{∘}$ and $x={338}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301557 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1069 "q-32173" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1071 "A partitioned matrix $M$ contains the following blocks: \n\n- ${M}_{12},$ which is a $2\\times 1$ block\n\n- ${M}_{21},$ which is a $2\\times 3$ block\n\n- ${M}_{22},$ which is a $2\\times 1$ block\n\nWhat are the dimensions of ${M}_{11}?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1072 #uuid "9c49211b-ae4b-51fe-8084-ee8c6ceee565" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302086 1074 "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}?\\times ? & 2\\times 1 \\\\ 2\\times 3 & 2\\times 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{11}$ must be a $2\\times 3$ block." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302028 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1069 "q-32176" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1072 #uuid "587dcbe8-780f-5ae0-a952-bc8f4458a90a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302087 1074 "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301665 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1069 "q-32177" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1072 #uuid "1208e79a-a661-5205-bd4d-ce2da940348a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302088 1074 "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301245 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301818 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1069 "q-321831" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2=0.5$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1072 #uuid "31d57fe2-c1f3-5fbe-86e1-bada0f28fffa" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302089 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2 & =0.5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1.5)=-0.982\\,794…≈-1.0\n$$\n\nSince $-1.0$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\pi +(-0.982\\,794…)≈2.2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=2\\pi +(-0.982\\,794…)≈5.3.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.2$ and $x=5.3.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301648 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301922 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1069 "q-321873" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1071 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1072 #uuid "8f70fa2d-b205-5ec4-8959-1f99a515e43e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302090 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)={0}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+{0}^{∘}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=180\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301377 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1069 "q-321877" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1071 "![](images/36/369954f7abfabce27d112287ed232ce9a3a7e75ce993bba6300203777442f8fc.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(2,2),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1072 #uuid "521c20c6-8032-5c25-9cc6-c7967c4bc8d6" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302091 1074 "The angles that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, its polar equation is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301397 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301412 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1069 "q-321879" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1071 "Find the **exact solutions in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1072 #uuid "e6f26b9c-d6be-5728-81f7-ec89b388ce38" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302092 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301392 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301464 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1069 "q-321883" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=7$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1072 #uuid "32753b91-f55d-52f4-911f-bdd1a5b14895" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302093 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(7)=1.428\\,899…≈1.4\n$$\n\nSince $1.4$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.4.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\pi +{x}_{1}=-\\pi +1.428\\,899…≈-1.7.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=-1.7$ and $x=1.4.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301792 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1069 "q-321889" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1071 "![](images/e6/e64215da53594d7e7bc24f8df953aa84f0648cd3ab58d0a8b8005be653c28902.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n {{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1072 #uuid "1da829fe-35c5-5340-bbe7-532291b5385d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302094 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the second quadrant. So, we find the angle in the second quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301425 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301444 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1069 "q-321890" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1071 "![](images/b5/b51c85930a5b93bcf7f3e7537cd39cf4134d93470b580c0f7276ddb58ade7297.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1072 #uuid "6149e2ab-873c-5575-a1df-2ba15ff46c49" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302095 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=4.$ Substituting $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301405 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301833 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1069 "q-321891" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1071 "![](images/da/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1072 #uuid "27209947-9b17-5ce0-95a6-938cd5c30020" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302096 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=10.$ Substituting $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301568 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301737 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1069 "q-321896" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1071 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1072 #uuid "b9b8c91c-7f56-5eed-b909-9129ac16f87f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302097 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.5)=-0.463\\,647…≈-0.5\n$$\n\nSince $-0.5$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.5$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +(-0.463\\,647…)=2.677\\,945…≈2.7.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=-0.5$ and $x=2.7.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301330 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1069 "q-321898" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1071 "![](images/b0/b07f18c13e3bca94d2b55d61b4a63b2a294d0e47ed8a2853f5e10c5140047b27.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},-3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1072 #uuid "0393dc3c-4161-5478-9a2f-cbfba3f335eb" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302098 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301430 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301706 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1069 "q-321918" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1071 "![](images/9a/9af86b077257cd7a6052de0a0a89d720935eace0ac87dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1072 #uuid "9029a697-8d69-532b-b921-5f4702dab115" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302099 1074 "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301882 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1069 "q-321924" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1071 "What is the quotient of $-14{x}^{3}+43{x}^{2}-50x+17$ divided by $2{x}^{2}-5x+3?$\n\nThe quotient is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1072 #uuid "a0e345ab-8592-5155-9ac1-0aba0a68c2ce" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302100 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-7x+4 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-5x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-14{x}^{3}+43{x}^{2}-50x+17\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-14{x}^{3}+35{x}^{2}-21x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8{x}^{2}-29x+17 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{8{x}^{2}-20x+12}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-9x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $-7x+4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-9x+5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301798 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1069 "q-321926" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1071 "What is the quotient of $15{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-15x-11$ divided by $3{x}^{2}-x-4?$\n\nThe quotient is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1072 #uuid "a66216d9-156d-58af-8d03-55beb5f24515" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302101 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x+2 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}-x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,15{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-15x-11\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{15{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-20x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+5x-11 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-2x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $5x+2,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $7x-3.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301749 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301874 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1069 "q-321939" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1071 "![](images/46/460b94dd8d68c7ed7e5bab45befb9c86496291ae7f192ab022e47493807525d5.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-3,4)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,4).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1072 #uuid "390af415-dcf0-5200-af38-71372e9b2ef3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302102 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $5$ centered at the point $(-3,4).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301625 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301669 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1069 "q-321942" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1071 "![](images/59/59cef258a1ad0e6a0f56c37e0b7020e5341c329d5655c41f49e6e78eafca8b3f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-5,-2)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1,-2).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1072 #uuid "9bce5484-c85d-583f-82f3-6100e4f480f5" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302103 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301327 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301935 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1069 "q-321947" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1071 "![](images/11/11f1e6651d86e0b00884091338029395cd6d2ff44f25b97d010ec750d13bef59.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1072 #uuid "ea11e213-e469-58c8-9667-84ff5e30c91a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302104 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/2a/2a281b909e4a3577ea3774e9d75c43d97e4d4a69d0f673eed4189834e1ed1c43.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301655 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301913 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1069 "q-321948" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1071 "![](images/ed/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1072 #uuid "849c57f2-d360-5ce8-a933-fa42e7401299" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302105 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/68/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301272 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301592 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1069 "q-321970" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1071 "![](images/5e/5e29d99fc6ae3a848bfe59ab9e77595ab9ca2a605d0d03139e8599adf15e6fd9.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=9$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1072 #uuid "1036ad5f-680e-537b-8426-836a619b5091" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302106 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301752 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301884 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1069 "q-321972" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1071 "![](images/6a/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1072 #uuid "53021125-fcea-5da1-87cd-be2698ee5417" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302107 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301687 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301897 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1069 "q-321976" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1071 "![](images/fb/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1072 #uuid "d06f28ce-407e-57f3-9468-51dcff74f6bf" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302108 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/28/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301636 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301972 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301985 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1069 "q-321986" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1072 #uuid "7267f9b7-cdd2-51d4-b411-b6d5bc974cb9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302109 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301429 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301490 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301835 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1069 "q-321988" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1072 #uuid "c298ac38-ce78-5be9-baf7-f23482f91da4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302110 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301491 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301919 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301941 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1069 "q-321989" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1072 #uuid "553d8835-419a-5f16-a1a2-9829041916d7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302111 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301458 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301542 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302044 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1069 "q-321991" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +13,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1072 #uuid "dd0e867b-d505-5af8-8b82-05070f856f71" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302112 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301576 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302007 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1069 "q-322283" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1071 "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1072 #uuid "260fee5a-9ecb-5e89-bd59-2ddce1d08a7d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302113 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/5a/5af1febb2c8dbf1b19ee64addd8f8ccdd5a30f9330d36ab800475fce9f6740c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ and $b=2\\pi .$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301979 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1069 "q-32244" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+2y=5 \\\\  & -10x-10y=-25 \\\\  & 8x+8y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1072 #uuid "cbdb5209-5ae6-5fea-a525-87f604e2410b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302114 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301268 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1069 "q-32245" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & 4x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1072 #uuid "534e2a93-e71a-5475-894e-dfd5ca99a3a6" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302115 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & (k+2)y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302018 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1069 "q-322566" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1071 "![](images/02/024a7e545d37295878d7f32efd393310dcf1c2c9f88f124ffc5fe46b05155e2c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1072 #uuid "f1cf4030-03f9-5725-9abc-7ed81f76c1a6" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302116 1074 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301271 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301695 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1069 "q-322568" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1071 "![](images/7e/7e92a09f8448f4d320f86656bc0774e6167b96156041c32eb7a64ba5e8820128.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=3:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1072 #uuid "cf39b4a5-b11b-542b-bc1c-fed45785b019" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302117 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{3}{7}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}d+\\frac{3}{7}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301664 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301894 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1069 "q-322574" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1071 "![](images/2a/2a9cacfc6787e60229076c4abd2854709d51ac718c8d519256a61dbe92852b05.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{GH}$ in the ratio $GJ:JH=6:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ via the vectors $g$ and $h.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$g+$\n{{field-2}}\n$h$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1072 #uuid "ba11ec48-b1bc-5ad5-b2d2-084fb1f0daf5" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302118 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OGH,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\overset{\\to }{GO}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-g+h \\\\  & =h-g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $GJ:JH=6:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{GJ}=\\frac{6}{13}\\overset{\\to }{GH}=\\frac{6}{13}(h-g).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ} \\\\  & =g+\\frac{6}{13}(h-g) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}g+\\frac{6}{13}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301321 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301780 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1069 "q-322575" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1071 "![](images/d9/d9448f9f05666f9806975b37a73f09c492eb73139b6f9395b619f077f3a584c9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q,$ and $R$ divides $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR:RQ=4:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ via the vectors $p$ and $q.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OR}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$p+$\n{{field-2}}\n$q$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1072 #uuid "192ab3c2-7926-59cc-a0a7-34dbb7dda250" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302119 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-p+q \\\\  & =q-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(q-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+\\frac{4}{13}(q-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}p+\\frac{4}{13}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301632 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301859 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1069 "q-322576" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1071 "![](images/36/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1072 #uuid "4310688d-8e77-5533-8343-0450b5396777" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302120 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overse