& =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{3\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n,\\,x={315}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=45\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=315\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ and $b=360\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301552 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301753 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301930 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1069 "q-322666" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1071 "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1072 #uuid "2781cad1-5251-5d9e-95c8-df9b823a321e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302127 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301289 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301470 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301741 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1069 "q-322670" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1071 "Express $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1072 #uuid "5ad8e92c-0ebd-5c39-8d19-d9dce92fcbc9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302128 1074 "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5-11\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+121} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=-11$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-11}{5}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{11}{5}) \\\\  & ≈5.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{146}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.14)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.14))\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301244 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301332 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301496 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1069 "q-322671" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1071 "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+\\,i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1072 #uuid "cf8141f3-3d6b-54e7-81c4-c29188080b02" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302129 1074 "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\sqrt{3}>0$ and $y=-1<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301768 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1069 "q-322701" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1071 "![](images/e0/e03611d72e1c88d1e49806ede117b6808186de27346e426104dca69391f29bbf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1072 #uuid "1f9978e0-d89e-56b3-a4e1-ec22c1dfd203" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302130 1074 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301322 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301374 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1069 "q-322774" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1071 "Express $\\frac{12{x}^{3}-20x+96}{2{x}^{2}-3x+5}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{2{x}^{2}-3x+5},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $12{x}^{3}-20x+96$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{2}-3x+5$ | __ |  |  | $2{x}^{2}-3x+5$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1072 #uuid "ffc55b0b-d2bb-5785-9bad-343d08c921b4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302131 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+9 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-3x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,12{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}-20x+96\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{12{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+30x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,18{x}^{2}-50x+96 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,18{x}^{2}-27x+45}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-23x+51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $6x+9,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-23x+51.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{12{x}^{3}-20x+96}{2{x}^{2}-3x+5}=6x+9+\\frac{(-23x+51)}{2{x}^{2}-3x+5}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301236 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1069 "q-32280" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1072 #uuid "591da14b-a9b8-58b9-a31f-95d22b99b938" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302132 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the above:\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\pi )=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(-1)-0 \\\\ -6\\cdot 1 \\\\ -4\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301547 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1069 "q-322923" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1071 "What is the quotient of $-4{x}^{4}+21{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+7$ divided by $-{x}^{2}+4x+3?$\n\nThe quotient is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1072 #uuid "6022302d-54a6-517d-bb60-6887afcf8323" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302133 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-5x+2 \\\\  & -{x}^{2}+4x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-4{x}^{4}+21{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+0x+7\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{4}+16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5{x}^{3}-22{x}^{2}+0x+7 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}-15x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2{x}^{2}+15x+7 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2{x}^{2}+8x+6}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $4{x}^{2}-5x+2,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $7x+1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301803 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301927 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1069 "q-322924" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1071 "Express $\\frac{-8{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+24x-4}{4{x}^{2}-3x+2}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{4{x}^{2}-3x+2},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $-8{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+24x-4$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4{x}^{2}-3x+2$ | __ |  |  | $4{x}^{2}-3x+2$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1072 #uuid "6f766b1c-625d-564c-a96e-40e056676d17" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302134 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2{x}^{2}+x-5 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-3x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-8{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+24x-4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,8{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-23{x}^{2}+24x-4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,20{x}^{2}+22x-4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,20{x}^{2}+15x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $-2{x}^{2}+x-5,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $7x+6.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+24x-4}{4{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =-2{x}^{2}+x-5+\\frac{7x+6}{4{x}^{2}-3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301802 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1069 "q-32300" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1071 "Given that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k,$ \n$c=i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k,$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ what is the value of $u?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1072 #uuid "069ed041-ef82-5d0f-a684-e05f430b2167" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302135 1074 "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}+u-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $u=0.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302016 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1069 "q-323123" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1071 "| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P(-1<Y\\le 1)$ given that the random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n$P(-1<Y\\le 1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1072 #uuid "7ea77d7f-2017-5e3a-84ce-52926f172192" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302136 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<Y\\le 1) & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=0)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301241 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301334 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1069 "q-323153" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx-4=-\\frac{2}{5}\\,t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{4}{25}\\,{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1072 #uuid "599665ce-994a-5a05-a433-000c5a08e621" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302137 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4),\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1) \\\\ {(x-4)}^{2} & =-(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301359 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301481 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1069 "q-323209" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1072 #uuid "cfbb5ea9-8628-5c66-9846-4b5cf013f95b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302138 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301949 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1069 "q-32321" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}-3X+1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1072 #uuid "997c432b-610d-53fe-ad2b-73f9ef5c7989" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302139 1074 "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}-3A+I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}-3A+I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10-(-6)+1 & 6-18 \\\\ 1-3 & 15-9+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301633 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1069 "q-323215" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=16{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+7x+12$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1072 #uuid "7782f6dc-5bfd-598b-a7bf-b3050e3b4fc9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302140 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 6,$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 8,$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{16}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{16},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\  & \\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $28$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 14=28.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301391 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302043 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1069 "q-323216" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y-3=5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1072 #uuid "254255ae-484c-589a-98c3-50d15ffe156c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302141 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =x,\\,t=\\frac{y-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25} & =x \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =25x \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =25x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301553 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1069 "q-323217" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-7x+5$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1072 #uuid "fd405f56-1515-597c-aca2-d76da5d999e9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302142 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301452 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1069 "q-323218" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1072 #uuid "fd8abfc9-fc42-5fbe-8914-ffe40a38eb8b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302143 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301624 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301861 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1069 "q-323221" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=4{t}^{2},\\,y-1=8t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1072 #uuid "40102566-3303-5239-a703-c00789ea4ec7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302144 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+2}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-1}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+2}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{x+2}{4} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =64\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{4}) \\\\ {(y-1)}^{2} & =16(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301646 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1069 "q-323222" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-5x-9$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1072 #uuid "76cf11e5-5176-5390-9ae1-3e49263bcd6b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302145 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 3$, and $\\pm 9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{9}{5}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301477 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301690 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1069 "q-323229" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1072 #uuid "0901ad83-94a2-5f71-b9b0-e0800a27b5d3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302146 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301361 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301776 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1069 "q-323230" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1072 #uuid "fdca0494-e6b5-50e8-8782-269f2de139c3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302147 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4(x+1),\\,t=-(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-(y-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(y-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(x+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+1) \\\\ {(y-2)}^{2} & =-4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301911 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1069 "q-323231" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-5x+10$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1072 #uuid "20f1ea6e-66f7-58d3-8316-8fa7e10b31b6" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302148 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{10}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 5, & \\pm 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301614 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1069 "q-32331" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ 8x+ky=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1072 #uuid "f5f8dda9-f72e-566e-91bc-44ecdce08b8e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302149 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y=2 \\\\ (k-2)y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301657 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301938 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301990 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1069 "q-323610" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1071 "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-10x-8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,5).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1072 #uuid "44f388d6-5078-5e1a-a0fb-9aeba444134a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302150 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301439 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301641 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301834 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1069 "q-323819" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1071 "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1072 #uuid "3518da80-0db9-569e-b980-2b5efc57c5f4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302151 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301424 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301969 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1069 "q-323833" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({15}^{∘})=2-\\sqrt{3}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1072 #uuid "58b5a06d-6765-5f58-9d98-7e98880563e0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302152 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(2-\\sqrt{3})={15}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301783 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302032 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1069 "q-323836" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1072 #uuid "3e989feb-9d82-58b4-8c8e-96d5e37a6d7a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302153 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301727 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301728 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1069 "q-323843" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+2}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({75}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3}+2.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1072 #uuid "9f1c2e80-bcc9-5d9e-9477-56e077c4e55e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302154 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+2)={75}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301587 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301970 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1069 "q-323844" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({157.5}^{∘})=1-\\sqrt{2}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1072 #uuid "ac7bfba0-750c-59a2-819b-59513772943a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302155 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1-\\sqrt{2})={157.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={157.5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301968 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302002 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1069 "q-323846" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1072 #uuid "677209be-7018-5c94-a993-4bf063c74a74" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302156 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing this as $x=\\theta +{180}^{∘}\\,n,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301308 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301431 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301659 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301877 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301986 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1069 "q-323879" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1072 #uuid "a3d03c74-03cc-5bc6-84c9-29bb98c3c0ef" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302157 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-\\frac{5}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}-4\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-5+\\frac{13}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301560 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301668 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302014 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1069 "q-324056" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1071 "![](images/6f/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1072 #uuid "58a0a3a9-3419-5548-9c90-737710ae4aa7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302158 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301333 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301380 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301660 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1069 "q-324057" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1071 "![](images/43/435dd125850e3f97b5c3753a027f2db8718131ab499ef6711eaac637b1a022bd.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=1.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1072 #uuid "4ff70978-9a65-58ca-9d6e-6cfce9ac09ea" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302159 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301240 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301744 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1069 "q-324126" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}<0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1072 #uuid "d7d1c524-722a-5a9b-8143-1856821a68f0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302160 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{11-4x}{3-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=\\frac{11}{4}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into three subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4}),\\,(\\frac{11}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{11}{4})$ | $(\\frac{11}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11-4x$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(\\frac{11}{4},3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)<0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,\\frac{11}{4},\\,3).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301591 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1069 "q-324128" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1072 #uuid "856142f4-de92-591a-b38f-6186187501e3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302161 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =1((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{9}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{17}{8}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301571 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1069 "q-32416" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1071 "Calculate the second derivative of  $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,{s}^{4}+10s,\\,55⟩$ with respect to $s.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1072 #uuid "f8539782-987e-5a96-b997-ff8444e6065b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302162 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{4}+10s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(55)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,4{s}^{3}+10,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the second derivative by differentiating ${f}^{′}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4{s}^{3}+10),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,12{s}^{2},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301277 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1069 "q-32417" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1071 "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡3t\\,i-5{t}^{4}\\,j+\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1072 #uuid "0d5868b7-9316-570b-b593-afbcd34e82e0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302163 1074 "We find the derivative of $f(t)$  by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡3t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301605 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301635 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301790 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1069 "q-324223" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{4+2\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (cos$ \n$(${{field-2}}$)$\n$+\\,isin$\n$(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1072 #uuid "84132fd7-313c-5312-aa24-f8eea348cc26" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302164 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{4+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3-5\\text{i})(4-2\\text{i})}{(4+2\\text{i})(4-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6\\text{i}-20\\text{i}+10{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{4}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-26\\text{i}-10}{16-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-26\\text{i}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{13}{10}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{13}{10}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{13}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+169}{100}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{170}{100}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{10}$ and $y=-\\frac{13}{10},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{13}{10}}{\\frac{1}{10}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(13) \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -1.49 \\\\  & ≈4.79,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.79)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.79))\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301653 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301784 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301976 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1069 "q-324266" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1071 "![](images/70/70115efec4ac9137d101549afd2dae2ffe436d81d01a08bb241c09f4a5896802.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-7x+12\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1072 #uuid "a0711ded-639d-5ff5-80fc-a7356841c90b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302165 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-7x+12\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/54/5423f579b521506be23a372cb13f2e2c6593e1efd822600f153d76a94fef86ce.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le 3$ or $x\\ge 4.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301279 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1069 "q-324311" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1071 "The durations of direct flights on a certain route are normally distributed with a mean of $180$ minutes and a standard deviation of $20$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes? **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.4960\\,$ | $\\,0.4920\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.4960\\,$ | $\\,0.4920\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes is approximately\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1072 #uuid "5107c5fd-8639-5bbf-a780-c1226b0410f7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302166 1074 "Let $X$ represent the flight duration (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(180,{20}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(178<X<188).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(178<X<188) & =P(\\frac{178-180}{20}<Z<\\frac{188-180}{20}) \\\\  & =P(-0.1<Z<0.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.1<Z<0.4) & =P(Z<0.4)-P(Z\\le -0.1) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.4)-P(Z\\le -0.1) \\\\  & =Φ(0.4)-Φ(-0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.4) & =0.6554 \\\\ Φ(-0.1) & =0.4602.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.1<Z<0.4) & =Φ(0.4)-Φ(-0.1) \\\\  & =0.6554-0.4602 \\\\  & =0.1952 \\\\  & ≈0.20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected flight lasts more than $178$ minutes but less than $188$ minutes is approximately $0.20.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301595 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1069 "q-324314" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1071 "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1072 #uuid "6829b460-7921-509d-b187-5e5360672369" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302167 1074 "Let $X$ represent the rod length (in centimeters). Then we have $X∼N(100,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(96.8\\le X<102.4).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(96.8\\le X<102.4) & =P(\\frac{96.8-100}{4}\\le Z<\\frac{102.4-100}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60) & =P(Z<0.60)-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.60)-P(Z\\le -0.80) \\\\  & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.60) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.80) & =0.2119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80<Z<0.60) & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =0.7257-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.5138 \\\\  & ≈0.51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately $0.51.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301686 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301709 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301860 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1069 "q-324342" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality  $\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1072 #uuid "0f02dc68-54f4-5228-b22e-768099e2b502" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302168 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Since the function equals zero at $x=-5$ and at $x=2,$ we include these endpoints in the solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in [\\,-5,-1\\,)⋃[\\,2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301500 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302040 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1069 "q-324344" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1071 "Express $z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1072 #uuid "215c16c3-5948-5417-9503-ddb002423a5f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302169 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302037 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1069 "q-324349" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1071 "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1072 #uuid "aace1ce7-1675-5a50-8ee4-f15b9647304c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302170 1074 "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301365 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301726 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1069 "q-324392" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1072 #uuid "9ac6cfc5-8412-5ead-ae4f-e70cb354576c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302171 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{3}{4})≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301343 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301418 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1069 "q-324396" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1072 #uuid "5c36479a-53a2-504d-8cbf-7d29626fa8b0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302172 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{78.463}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/e0/e05c5b50dc8308d278cb6dac3408349da1865d37601003e0ee3025c937404239.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{360}^{∘}-{78.463}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{281.537}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=78.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}$\nand $x=281.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301461 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301963 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1069 "q-324397" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1072 #uuid "442bcaa4-6366-559d-86b7-c0022ac3924b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302173 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2.3})≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/97/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301389 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301710 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1069 "q-324401" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1072 #uuid "ac93d942-8a4e-5c8d-b866-cfb74d41e336" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302174 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301494 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1069 "q-324458" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1071 "In electronics manufacturing, the average resistance of a certain type of resistor is $100\\,Ω.$ However, $5%$ of resistors have resistance no more than $96.5\\,Ω.$ Assuming that resistor resistances are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the resistance, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.25$ | $0.20$ | $0.15$ | $0.10$ | $0.05$ | $0.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$Ω$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1072 #uuid "c76de2b6-dcfd-5626-8f87-83f3745840b1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302175 1074 "Let $X$ represent the resistance (in ohms). Then we have $X∼N(100,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 96.5)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 96.5) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{96.5-100}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.5}{1.6449} \\\\  & ≈2.13\\,Ω.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301544 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1069 "q-32499" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1071 "Differentiate $2g(t)-h(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3t-3,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-t,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1072 #uuid "5ce8c747-438b-5a4a-8cfe-ec69b8a216ec" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302176 1074 "Using the constant multiple and the sum rules, we have\n\n$$\n(2g(t)-h(t))^{′}=2{g}^{′}(t)-{h}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3,\\,-2t⟩ \\\\ {h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2g(t)-h(t))^{′} & =2{g}^{′}(t)-{h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3,\\,-2t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,6,\\,-4t⟩-⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6-(-1),\\,-4t-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,7,\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301561 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1069 "q-32502" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1071 "If  $f(u)=u\\,i+2u\\,j+3u\\,k$ and $u(t)={t}^{2}$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1072 #uuid "5d2dc74e-c392-56dc-bab3-713a1d0cef87" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302177 1074 "Using the chain rule, we can write \n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}u\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}2u\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}3u\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j+3\\,k)2t \\\\  & =2t\\,i+4t\\,j+6t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302012 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1069 "q-32503" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1071 "Differentiate $f(t)+g(t)+h(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨-t,\\,0,t⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨-{t}^{2},0,{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1072 #uuid "f67b45fc-cd86-5ee0-8299-de9e4060f565" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302178 1074 "Using the sum rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f(t)+g(t)+h(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t)+{h}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ {h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+g(t)+h(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t)+{h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩+⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t-1,\\,0,\\,2t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302003 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1069 "q-325105" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1071 "What is the quotient of $-6{x}^{4}+11{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+20x-18$ divided by $2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-5?$\n\nThe quotient is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1072 #uuid "d273473e-dd88-508e-aca6-a6249f67fd14" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302179 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x+4 \\\\  & 2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-6{x}^{4}+11{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+20x-18\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-18 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+12x-20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5{x}^{2}-7x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $-3x+4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $5{x}^{2}-7x+2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301497 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1069 "q-325106" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1071 "What is the quotient of $-2{x}^{4}+11{x}^{3}-17{x}^{2}+3x+3$ divided by $-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+1?$\n\nThe quotient is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1072 #uuid "e902eb71-f118-50ae-bfee-7615e9d48833" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302180 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-3 \\\\  & -{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{4}+11{x}^{3}-17{x}^{2}+3x+3\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+2x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{3}-13{x}^{2}+x+3 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+6x-3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}-5x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $2x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-{x}^{2}-5x+6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301349 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301734 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1069 "q-325107" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1071 "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1072 #uuid "a7e25bbd-62e2-55a9-9433-daf79eb00d75" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302181 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/17/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302031 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1069 "q-32511" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1071 "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k$ \nand \n$c={e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1072 #uuid "abace35f-02da-57bf-9b60-2e1f0caa0d18" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302182 1074 "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})+{e}^{-2}\\cdot {e}^{2}-{e}^{2}\\cdot (2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & =e-1+1-2e+3 \\\\  & =3-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301276 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1069 "q-325110" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1071 "What is the remainder of $-6{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+6x+2$ divided by $3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+5x-4?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1072 #uuid "0daf2810-8b21-5a33-a2a3-5dad72a7a14f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302183 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x+1 \\\\  & 3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+5x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-6{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+6x+2\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-\\,2x+2 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+5x-4}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-7x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the quotient is $-2x+1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $4{x}^{2}-7x+6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301304 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301868 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1069 "q-325111" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1071 "Express $\\frac{-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder of the division.$$\n\n| $-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ | __ |  |  | $3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1072 #uuid "b2478f21-c553-56a7-b300-ecaea7cc5588" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302184 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x+5 \\\\  & 3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,6{x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,15{x}^{3}-26{x}^{2}+9x+13 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{15{x}^{3}-25{x}^{2}+5x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+4x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $-2x+5,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-{x}^{2}+4x-7.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-6{x}^{4}+25{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}+x+13}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4}=-2x+5\\,+\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{2}+4x-7)}{3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x+4}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301419 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301708 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1069 "q-325113" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1072 #uuid "9d3bce2a-acb5-5009-b608-6cf97a06fc87" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302185 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $x={120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/29/29560b074afbb7d4e0cde3c1964e14696ef9879618e42c0b0c41f478e7ffcb72.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the third quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-120\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=120\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301519 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1069 "q-325116" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1071 "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1072 #uuid "d53565bf-d2c4-5e5a-8659-e7dab8f5253a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302186 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301246 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301504 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1069 "q-325125" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1072 #uuid "eab8e489-594d-52e1-8e07-a54c47bfe885" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302187 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301966 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1069 "q-325129" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1071 "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1072 #uuid "d7053778-cd34-5d92-bb60-d0cacb0e2655" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302188 1074 "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301387 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1069 "q-325138" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1071 "![](images/f5/f561603cc7224046f500c4f983e804b1970a1b2b74a61c6863aceee69d48e471.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the curve shown above is $r=${{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1072 #uuid "0fdf4dc7-9b49-5173-bf61-b0d2f648b0f3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302189 1074 "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $13$ centered at the origin. \n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=13.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301851 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1069 "q-325140" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1071 "![](images/84/84259f18d3d050bf6c7b0784250e873c92f065fc60a4ca4fa890bcbab1481aeb.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the curve shown above is $r=${{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1072 #uuid "db7d0364-c806-5ef3-b22f-95c5aba55da2" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302190 1074 "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $7.5$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=7.5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301483 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1069 "q-325143" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1071 "What is the remainder of $-4{x}^{3}+16$ divided by $2{x}^{2}-3x+5?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1072 #uuid "e563c3b5-1035-5672-9ab4-671fa924b598" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302191 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{3}+16=-4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x+16\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-3 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-3x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x+6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{2}+10x+16 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,6{x}^{2}+9x-15}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-2x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $x+31.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301338 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301663 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1069 "q-325149" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1071 "Express $\\frac{4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x+2$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{2}-x+2$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1072 #uuid "929e38a0-4113-56b0-9230-18b8b2084dc0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302192 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3\\,=\\,4{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+0x+3\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+4x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+0x+3\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+0x+3 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,{x}^{2}-8x+3 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,{x}^{2}+x-2}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,9x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $4{x}^{2}+4x-1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-9x+5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}+3}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,}=\\,4{x}^{2}+4x-1\\,+\\frac{\\,(-9x+5)\\,}{\\,{x}^{2}-x+2\\,}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301725 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301878 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1069 "q-325150" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1071 "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ | __ |  |  | ${x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1072 #uuid "ad962629-0b91-5e68-b607-3e8b45d5534d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302193 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1\\,=\\,2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+0x+1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x+1 \\\\  & {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+0x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}+7x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $2x+1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $3{x}^{2}+7x+5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1}{\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4\\,} & =2x+1+\\frac{3{x}^{2}+7x+5}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301564 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301846 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1069 "q-325151" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1071 "Express $\\frac{-9{x}^{3}+22x}{\\,-3x+4\\,}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{\\,-3x+4\\,},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $-9{x}^{3}+22x$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3x+4$ | __ |  |  | $-3x+4$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1072 #uuid "e3920c97-b4ab-59fe-ba64-09eb11fa08ac" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302194 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ term and the constant term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n-9{x}^{3}+22x\\,=\\,-9{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+22x+0\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}+4x-2 \\\\  & -3x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,-9{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+22x+0\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-9{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-12{x}^{2}+22x+0 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,12{x}^{2}+16x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+0 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,6x-8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $3{x}^{2}+4x-2,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $8.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-9{x}^{3}+22x}{\\,-3x+4\\,} & =3{x}^{2}+4x-2+\\frac{8}{-3x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301328 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302022 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1069 "q-325154" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1071 "Find the **Cartesian coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis.\n\nThe point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1072 #uuid "30f62290-05c5-54c1-8968-f2a46b935b10" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302195 1074 "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{5}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{13}{5}),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/86/86158ced718c422c9b02d620ca4544eb7d2ff0e110a04fe4b7f749160fc46649.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $(0,\\frac{13}{5}\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301702 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301875 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1069 "q-325160" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1071 "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ intersects the negative $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1072 #uuid "6804e529-a43d-5a85-a338-641666526279" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302196 1074 "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{2}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/23/2399108905ce184b2b2d2d299f5e52539af01228b484316ece8b2efdd6a3b852.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{13}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301369 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301569 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1069 "q-325162" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1071 "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1072 #uuid "34aa541d-528e-5947-8566-8089c39f0390" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302197 1074 "The polar curve $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{23}{6}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cab0604a3a8a45de244519cd407d310e8add3978e13cd20e1ef6b3d38e191fd.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{23}{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301248 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1069 "q-325168" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1071 "![](images/2a/2abff4f1dcb5bfd305a5560293892828f94d3fe8cca755bec1998d4f3fda077f.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(5,12)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1072 #uuid "5085dc52-8abc-524e-9823-f057e2f14cfb" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302198 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=13.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302039 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1069 "q-32517" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1072 #uuid "a1b8b6ae-30af-5f18-a21d-8dba558350b5" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302199 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301750 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1069 "q-325173" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1071 "![](images/fe/fecfcefdf82c8ae08dd8788d2a80df0a717c04344042bb38371c1dd5df95f670.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(2,-1)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1072 #uuid "bab73537-d026-53be-937c-1f3612e9e323" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302200 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301401 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1069 "q-325175" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1071 "![](images/82/82744601b054c3aba5f3d117291ccd52e3823db75d158532cbac89c31a99ff27.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(8,9)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1072 #uuid "de27953f-483f-570c-a3c4-6cbe07826955" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302201 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{145}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{145}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301992 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1069 "q-325177" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1071 "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(6,2)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1072 #uuid "c4937cfe-d61a-5ec6-948d-bfbd662e9f5a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302202 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{10}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301796 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1069 "q-325178" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1071 "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(1,7)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1072 #uuid "c47d4170-ba0c-50f9-a895-4f340ab17836" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302203 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=5\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301808 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1069 "q-32522" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1071 "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1072 #uuid "6ba28ff2-6afb-5aba-aec7-2e0d7e59bdc3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302204 1074 "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301937 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1069 "q-325317" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1071 "Express $[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )]^{4}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )]^{4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1072 #uuid "bfe36ae6-76ff-51a7-88a7-cb83cb274e1a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302205 1074 "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=4$ and $\\phi =5\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )]^{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 5\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301801 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1069 "q-325322" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{10}}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{10}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1072 #uuid "e9b0cf42-1e30-56e2-bde4-6e7b9b963d3d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302206 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{10}}={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-10}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-10$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{10}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-10} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-10\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-10\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301819 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1069 "q-325325" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}},$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1072 #uuid "f230b000-3682-570a-8639-c69b3241d8ca" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302207 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}}=3{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-4$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}} & =3{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\theta )]=3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301812 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1069 "q-32533" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1072 #uuid "912ad134-e874-55d5-a192-6e414d5ad134" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302208 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301249 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1069 "q-325334" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1071 "Express $[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]^{6}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]^{6}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1072 #uuid "1cbbd45b-5f34-53f7-ba2f-e416de409de2" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302209 1074 "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=6$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]^{6}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301677 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1069 "q-325338" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1071 "Express $[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )]^{8}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )]^{8}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1072 #uuid "193bbc7d-2acb-58cc-892d-84ea335e72f3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302210 1074 "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=8$ and $\\phi =6\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )]^{8} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\cdot 6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot 6\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(48\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(48\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301722 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1069 "q-325342" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]^{5}}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]^{5}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1072 #uuid "fc806522-282b-5112-900a-9e3f23fe10f5" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302211 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{5}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-5$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-5}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\theta )\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )-[-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301973 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1069 "q-325344" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]^{6}}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1072 #uuid "cd9539bf-438e-57f6-9e0c-3c22abe09efe" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302212 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-6$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta )\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-[-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301771 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1069 "q-325346" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1071 "Express $\\frac{4}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]^{5}}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{4}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]^{5}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1072 #uuid "ffd5235f-f170-5cd0-83d0-124e300d0da1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302213 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{5}} & =4{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-5$ and $\\phi =4\\theta .$ Substituting in these 