\\\\ 9\\,[(y+3)^{2}-9]-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-81-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+3)^{2}}{108}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{108} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-3),\\,a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}})(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301616 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301871 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1069 "q-325439" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1072 #uuid "586f88cb-07e5-56af-b3a5-889f8ba2856f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302222 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+4y)-16({x}^{2}-2x)-124 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+2)^{2}-4]-16\\,[(x-1)^{2}-1]-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-16(x-1)^{2}+16-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-16(x-1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+2)^{2}}{144}-\\frac{16(x-1)^{2}}{144} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(1,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301673 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1069 "q-32548" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1072 #uuid "883a308b-5dbc-5683-ad84-c19d4f942e32" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302223 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301443 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1069 "q-32570" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1072 #uuid "74c73bef-8f9e-5d05-b33f-448bad8fb053" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302224 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-4+3(-\\frac{2}{3})=-6.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301684 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301914 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1069 "q-325754" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1072 #uuid "2353ac86-d4a2-518d-aadd-7e3be0db90ef" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302225 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{x(x+7)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-7$ and $x=0.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-7,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-7,0\\,).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301525 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302226 1069 "q-