in{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301849 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1069 "q-325818" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $5{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}=40$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1072 #uuid "0e6cc6d0-52fc-5e78-a798-0ae0623917f7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302228 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301842 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1069 "q-325821" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=20$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1072 #uuid "f6ffa896-a254-5036-b1f3-6c074806d269" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302229 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{20} & =\\frac{20}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301420 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301945 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1069 "q-325846" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,-\\sqrt{14}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1072 #uuid "1bf30272-c0b4-5eee-b418-28d5533437be" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302230 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301363 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301940 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1069 "q-325849" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1071 "![](images/e2/e2c0bc639d03c94056b07ce1cc42976b88494f312e318199cef73043bc711c74.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1072 #uuid "a5469791-f633-5c9f-9748-5725886c702a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302231 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,3)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301396 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301944 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1069 "q-325854" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,+\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1072 #uuid "dbcc472f-5ffe-5c12-b68c-6360fd3ce661" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302232 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301565 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301603 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1069 "q-325860" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1072 #uuid "6faabf38-ea7b-5072-9ed7-46de60d9b5e2" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302233 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9} & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0).$\n\n![](images/5a/5a1377fa0ff46ff7a7e4b685d38852ad67abd8c0adfc432fc22d7c1d57b7a57f.png)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301270 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1069 "q-325892" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1072 #uuid "160175ff-95eb-5346-8c3a-941036db94a4" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302234 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{100}=10$ and $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301511 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1069 "q-325896" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,$\n$y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1072 #uuid "f2232c98-41b4-5732-a958-dab199f073f2" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302235 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{144}=12$ and $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301447 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1069 "q-325899" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=64$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1072 #uuid "747d48ad-be0e-5230-b3d4-35b386c3afac" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302236 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{64} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301513 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1069 "q-325900" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $25{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=225$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1072 #uuid "ab37bc7f-2a04-5418-b714-6d21fa630d85" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302237 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{25{x}^{2}}{225}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{225} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{9}=3$ and $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301954 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1069 "q-325901" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1071 "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}=16$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1072 #uuid "cf9f0c20-e02d-5520-8b06-a3b42b2a9347" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302238 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{8{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301473 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1069 "q-325905" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1072 #uuid "4df9edb0-31a2-5a49-8afc-8246406c7c24" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302239 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301258 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301704 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1069 "q-325916" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1071 "![](images/45/4561dbe8b730fa83f3044f7ab92bb5b2232e99b587269dbb31ffa463fd6ab348.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302240 1072 #uuid "deeecdb3-ccae-52cc-9ce4-d4c2af028726" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [1