⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302251 1072 #uuid "5eeeae17-7ba0-5384-9314-34b43700e9fe" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302251 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{10} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301847 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1069 "q-32597" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1072 #uuid "54aa3fc3-c0fb-5a79-b3e0-2e2c9dd3e2c8" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302252 1074 "We need to reduce the matrix to the row echelon form. For every elementary row operation we also write the equivalent notation in terms of multiplication by the corresponding elementary matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301286 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1069 "q-32598" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1072 #uuid "85e17ab0-6cd0-5554-bbc1-74b553196159" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302253 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-4.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301352 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301559 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302011 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1069 "q-325980" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1071 "![](images/7d/7db943c53e70d9b77490c2d73acac939be08fdfa4b0aa958addb2d50b6078543.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1072 #uuid "0b7882f7-bf9d-5ce2-89d4-f78e7c1ba739" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302254 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e0/e03130cdcae3fff94036453883eb638371b62a4ee26b9b550192aed32f95a39d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [