13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302256 1071 "![](images/5b/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire parabola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302256 1072 #uuid "f68a3cab-5c69-5c9f-96da-3899c8ad0a3f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302256 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6a/6ac31692404208d80637291fb30be028a7efdc3a172af0337da26039f1e87326.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301545 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301574 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1069 "q-325991" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=$ | $=$ | $-\\sqrt{5}\\,-\\,$ {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1072 #uuid "74967a64-b648-5512-8822-bc2f06be078f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302257 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(-\\frac{2}{3})-(-\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301257 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301446 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1069 "q-325997" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{159}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $325$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1072 #uuid "4a1bfb70-9351-599e-8d42-359c4bb40742" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302258 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{159}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{159}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe’re given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13})+(\\frac{24}{25})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}}{325}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{10}}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}-24\\sqrt{10}}{325}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301736 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301773 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301997 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1069 "q-326012" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1071 "![](images/76/76c3e62888071cf391a38616699388eaf093053924df9047ea457c7488865dbb.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-1)(x+5)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1072 #uuid "4795ff59-4206-5f9e-aa77-b6306633e46a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302259 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-1)(x+5)\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-1)(x+5)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4b9cd7557301e663b226e155bef4603d552b1c866411bc687ab4a082efeeaf5.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-5\\le x\\le 1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301303 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301432 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1069 "q-326013" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1071 "![](images/58/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1072 #uuid "8fc6062b-82cc-5523-a4f6-6f883f485c18" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302260 1074 "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301313 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301598 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1069 "q-326014" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1071 "![](images/de/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1072 #uuid "90a7567f-3548-5a15-bedd-9db11178758a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302261 1074 "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301293 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301707 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1069 "q-326018" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1071 "![](images/0c/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1072 #uuid "52ee4dd6-7ab9-5429-b0f8-a112547f6aa0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302262 1074 "![](images/56/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301263 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301740 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1069 "q-326022" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1071 "![](images/99/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1072 #uuid "d2bbedc4-0ff6-53bb-b9ae-7bd7218983f9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302263 1074 "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301640 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301764 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301777 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1069 "q-326026" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1071 "![](images/d4/d456a8bcd41477f36ce99230afafc9636f4d57b9c209de022bfcf2fc4ebb1a65.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x-1)(x+5)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1072 #uuid "20a8bf57-4bea-5f71-aa53-dad569ccf059" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302264 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+5)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x-1)(x+5)\n$$\n\n \nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/91/910856b0bf27df0038488fadfdb7dee8a8bd259ffbadf3a1f8c6f44f3a39cb3f.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-5<x<1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301383 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301793 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1069 "q-326042" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1072 #uuid "62d63af2-d66f-5fa9-a04a-0aa1ef3d6685" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302265 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6\\cdot {0}^{2}+12x+24\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & =0 \\\\ (2x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-\\frac{3}{2},0).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301799 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301867 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1069 "q-326043" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1072 #uuid "99df4a96-1be0-51fa-912f-796019bd7b76" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302266 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}+18x+32\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+18x+9 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[{x}^{2}+2x]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+1)^{2}-1]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}-9+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-1,0).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301329 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302041 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1069 "q-326046" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1072 #uuid "29b9babc-1ceb-5adb-98b3-9fd27d462a0a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302267 1074 "First, we define the function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $h(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $h(x)=0$ has no solution because ${x}^{2}+1>0$ for all real $x.$ On the other hand, $h(x)$ has a single vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ This asymptote divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $h(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $h(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $h(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $h(x)$ is positive on the interval $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nh(x)>0\\text{ for all }x\\in (\\,3,\\infty \\,).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301495 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301608 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301952 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1069 "q-326139" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1072 #uuid "b84dec9a-dbca-5159-a096-6bd8501547c8" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302268 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+3)(x+5)}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=-3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-3),\\,(-3,-2),\\,(-2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-3)$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $[-5,-3]$ and $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in [-5,-3\\,]\\cup (-2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301320 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301382 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301904 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1069 "q-326141" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1071 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1072 #uuid "aee0bf07-1691-5e80-899f-ef400a0f41d5" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302269 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-3)(x+1)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive  on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,]⋃[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301356 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301427 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301701 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1069 "q-326143" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1071 "![](images/95/9537dea9d30d2b4f45e2aa420f10860de86f7cd358f006855b1005b14c3ee22a.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x+4)(x-2)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1072 #uuid "74351edf-3ef1-5ed6-a28c-ad31d600ee3a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302270 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x+4)(x-2)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x+4)(x-2)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa1e361d53401bff39f4c53a2282d7b496ad9b46c586badc279265b2638ee73e.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -4$ or $x\\ge 2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301572 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1069 "q-326150" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.7\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1072 #uuid "5878b428-f5a6-5ebd-8385-5b72ac276f64" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302271 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301549 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1069 "q-32617" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1071 "Given that \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1072 #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302272 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301879 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1069 "q-326238" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1072 #uuid "6648c16d-423d-571d-89b3-28f10f5f61b7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302273 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301719 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1069 "q-326239" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1072 #uuid "26bb41ab-79e8-5c9f-95c2-ae20713f031a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302274 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301694 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302275 1068