tart by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t+1)-2 \\\\  & =2(t-1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2(t-1)^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301309 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301469 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301824 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1069 "q-326289" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1072 #uuid "231069bc-0578-5e9f-8f53-9a2feab4caea" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302285 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ 108\\,(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}) & =108 \\\\ 81+4(y-2)^{2} & =108 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & =27 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{27}{4} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,2-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301767 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301855 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301899 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301998 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1069 "q-326291" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1072 #uuid "b50067c4-fc94-5eda-819f-5e879abcd43d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302286 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=7 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the result to the  first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{2}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+3=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301358 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301662 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301772 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301858 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1069 "q-326294" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1072 #uuid "ad687230-8844-51e9-a75a-cfbd2d9890e7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302287 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{1}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301238 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301325 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301594 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301933 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1069 "q-326295" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1072 #uuid "2097b84a-e892-58a2-b3ad-b1e915fc3527" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302288 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{2}=10\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot 2=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301692 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301756 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301797 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301866 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1069 "q-326298" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1072 #uuid "3ba6f2a7-7dbc-5be9-8beb-c38e3acae4d1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302289 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{c}_{1}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301965 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1069 "q-32630" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1071 "If $h(x)=2{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}+6,$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1072 #uuid "f94a00c7-0509-5de0-b6ed-21806d8bb729" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302290 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{5}+3{y}^{4}-{y}^{3}+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{5}+3{y}^{4}-{y}^{3}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =10{y}^{4}+12{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{10{y}^{4}+12{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{10({h}^{-1}(x))^{4}+12({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-3({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{10({h}^{-1}(x))^{4}+12({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}-3({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301337 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301643 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301733 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301891 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1069 "q-326300" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1072 #uuid "d0d4a70a-5321-5bde-a4db-76e8b0c1223a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302291 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $3-\\frac{1}{2}t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 2.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}-1\\ge 2$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301312 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301441 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301482 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301774 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1069 "q-326304" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4t-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2}+3,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1072 #uuid "4a473799-9dcd-5e6d-9f92-4a1b0e38bad7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302292 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t+4)+4 \\\\  & =-(t-2)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-(t-2)^{2}+4\\le 4,$ we conclude that the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}+3\\ge 3.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301299 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301423 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301471 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301885 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1069 "q-326305" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}-4t,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+2,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1072 #uuid "d5a7ca62-b357-5afe-b367-a82218e19c1c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302293 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t+1)-2 \\\\  & =2(t-1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2(t-1)^{2}-2\\ge -2,$ we conclude that the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that $(t-1)^{2}+2\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301315 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301530 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301932 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1069 "q-326312" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,-2,-5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,1,-7⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y+z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1072 #uuid "d7278c5e-97e4-5ded-971c-857a998455ed" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302294 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-3,2,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =19i+7j+k \\\\  & =⟨19,7,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =⟨-3,2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨19,7,1⟩ & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z & =-39 \\\\ 19x+7y+z+39 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301580 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301950 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1069 "q-326334" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1071 "![](images/28/28c189efc805ec6aef299c4a89c1bb9299db7ad9f8544b86ed7509e6abc2e6a5.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1072 #uuid "c4832012-403b-56b1-80a4-399af628e376" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302295 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302019 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1069 "q-32635" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1071 "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1072 #uuid "2318ec9c-3e92-5221-b4ff-d2d8a492f82d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302296 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+5)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+5)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2y+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5) \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301596 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1069 "q-326363" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1072 #uuid "4b1dc201-81d1-5231-9601-4ced8e8e4925" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302297 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{85}-\\frac{45}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301273 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301755 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1069 "q-326378" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{3}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1072 #uuid "6128daa9-a33c-5d00-8017-59d8aba58347" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302298 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{50}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-24\\sqrt{3}}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301298 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1069 "q-32639" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1071 "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1072 #uuid "e2dac7d9-d918-58d0-a1d8-f6e51efc32ca" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302299 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6y-1)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301586 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301623 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301759 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1069 "q-326403" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1071 "![](images/82/82982dbd8325f28515ba723f1daeb8c09b9ce95e3731d5ce1e5964c2d9b3239e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}-8x-15\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1072 #uuid "dae44aa2-aefc-5485-b819-b1a120ca4934" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302300 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-8x-15\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-8x-15\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5e/5ee0cefdf30cacfb4906d547184449b24fd1a763f0094ca17a1f201c11bb8a9b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -5$ or $x\\ge -3.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301610 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301712 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301762 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1069 "q-326407" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1071 "![](images/8c/8c8872487af27f3d2e1e0ce862f861f2be2a4cf97c83858936155ca2a6b6e740.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}-6x-8\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1072 #uuid "9b5cd470-e8da-501c-8870-c11e53f8f859" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302301 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-6x-8\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-6x-8\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc265ca81928739c2154710d053e2ea19610861d6fdef65d49df497839a3c7d3.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -4$ or $x\\ge -2.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301284 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1069 "q-32643" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1071 "If $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡({x}^{2}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1072 #uuid "812a9224-9b4e-516e-80da-078aa909f4de" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302302 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={sin}^{3}⁡({x}^{2}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{2})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6y{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6y{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301292 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301326 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301476 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301791 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1069 "q-326442" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-{x}^{2}(y-2).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1072 #uuid "b96765d0-d865-5539-9831-e8b36b175473" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302303 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be201671c4862874082f9aa2bf598a19a629d808e263ac410629229bb97c8ebf.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc763b1ceba087be22a05c0e0f411d82e1a6ae13566f9c9625fa2e164d8be1ce.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{2}$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5b/5b2e7277212bacfb039b94783de1a669de6aed764ac8584c23f253448d4fc517.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301403 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301651 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301661 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301947 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1069 "q-326445" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302304 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{3}(y-1).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" 131