585 true] [17592186302305 1072 #uuid "8d9cdc30-17ca-57b2-8e37-cefab00d4e1d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302305 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/4244ec01c3ca7ea666967abfea565c5674f8740033f365b138a9488899b2017a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/4d/4d115bf92aa8255a17ee6e658026570c683d1bc65fc342463d7e66f58d4df554.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/91/91e3506692c75397376f3addbcab0636bb817462eec529206ba3a62c84d901be.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301794 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301841 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301903 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301982 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1069 "q-326449" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y-2).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1072 #uuid "eff8247b-82c6-57e6-95f7-54606e15b717" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302306 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bce3befee4d7ae90df59b7bfb17e965ca29615220ca6845a78cfaad3e02a0730.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/b6/b6cac24748d4a3990038ef7140943bd0947207456132e66ca6b7fb887be44164.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)^{2}$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-2,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/17/174965ef064b55b29326c4681f709a6dfcbc690673e2e5a7498e9a65c4a6af2c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301281 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301626 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301809 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1069 "q-326454" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1071 "![](images/92/925c86e195a98d7146400471d5f69fe6d46fd8a57042f2ece967cf59a8400ed6.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}+2x+15<0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x<$\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1072 #uuid "508f9342-ae06-5e1f-b3e3-9e96e00d44d3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302307 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+2x+15<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+2x+15\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/99/990d3828c9cf09da563aa59158afe6eafcb02635770de1a54029877d8f0a6f27.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-3$ or $x>5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301600 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301883 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1069 "q-326456" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1072 #uuid "6e6e34e6-b66f-59db-bc45-9af5966ce41c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302308 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}={210}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301890 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1069 "q-32646" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1071 "If $h(x)={2}^{x+1},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1072 #uuid "d457263d-a706-57aa-b637-8e6d867a6abd" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302309 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={2}^{x+1},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={2}^{y+1}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({2}^{y+1}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={2}^{y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{-y}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{2}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301880 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1069 "q-32647" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1072 #uuid "2e67ea89-60e8-5377-8f1d-5084c5f474ac" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302310 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=2 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301886 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1069 "q-32649" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1071 "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1072 #uuid "24196412-03d6-5e5f-92de-8b242613ba34" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302311 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-1$ and ${u}_{33}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=(-1)\\cdot 7=-7.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302035 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1069 "q-32652" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1071 "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $D=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $D$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}?$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1072 #uuid "b6b690f5-c73e-55f2-9a04-9d767e300105" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302312 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 12 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{21}=-2$ and ${u}_{22}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=(-2)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301422 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1069 "q-32662" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1071 "If $x=y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(8,4).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1072 #uuid "b3cc0c68-3553-59ec-9740-0e51c6886431" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302313 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(8,4),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(8,4)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{4}-4+2)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301742 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1069 "q-32671" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1072 #uuid "6904caba-1f35-5875-862d-7c3f71474ab1" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302314 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301310 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1069 "q-32685" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1072 #uuid "142d0252-9564-5605-b794-3e443fd3c338" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302315 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301290 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301378 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301573 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1069 "q-326901" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+2z+6=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,$\n{{field-2}}\n, \n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1072 #uuid "b8f8613f-ede8-5b23-9e9a-6a90b747b545" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302316 1074 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨2,-1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z+6 & =0 \\\\ z & =-x+\\frac{y}{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-3)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-3⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-3⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301528 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1069 "q-327046" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1071 "![](images/3d/3d34b42eb1874894af9675944e97e403d67db92bf96449fd8f87fbb14063a10e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1072 #uuid "b5c3e7c8-04d1-5bc5-af15-ec4125ed0196" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302317 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/c0/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301765 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1069 "q-327049" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1071 "![](images/a4/a43139fc26d5778c40db020a7ebc9122c2fd54c0dcd4eb44cfd1671936a4055a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1072 #uuid "d63f1783-5306-55e3-b17e-74d8a2e39050" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302318 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=\\frac{1}{2}a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301649 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1069 "q-32706" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1071 "If $x={y}^{5}+3y-5,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(33,2).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1072 #uuid "8588e3b0-fa4a-5436-801f-7b47fb73e332" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302319 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5}+3y-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3(1)+0 \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5{y}^{4}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(33,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(33,2)} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot {2}^{4}+3}=\\frac{1}{83}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301642 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301910 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1069 "q-327067" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}} and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1072 #uuid "0ccd729f-4b5c-5126-89b9-3434c059218b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302320 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{-\\sqrt{3}}=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301478 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301548 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1069 "q-327074" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1071 "![](images/c8/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1072 #uuid "1beeeaab-084b-5797-a939-fcb45aee542f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302321 1074 "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301888 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301995 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1069 "q-327075" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1071 "![](images/e6/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1072 #uuid "e61f5683-d5a7-570e-8d61-a9e450d0adae" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302322 1074 "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301499 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302036 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1069 "q-327078" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1071 "![](images/4c/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1072 #uuid "c302ea94-ce6a-528e-80d0-db3b0e2f4650" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302323 1074 "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m∦n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301345 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1069 "q-327111" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1071 "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ **NOT** evaluated during this iteration?" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1072 #uuid "bb4c52c9-56c1-5911-8844-218ed25165be" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302324 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,-2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only point where the slope is **NOT** evaluated is $(\\frac{3}{2},-2).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301942 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1069 "q-327132" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1072 #uuid "323aa95a-cd97-5a1e-8432-085c87b86469" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302325 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=-8.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301778 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302026 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1069 "q-327143" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1072 #uuid "8ef0e811-88f7-5ed2-9409-b69042af046a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302326 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[{y}^{2}+2y]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+1)^{2}-1]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}-4+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-1).$\n\n![](images/ef/efaa5f474c5f505f6a1ca98469a77f8d21e059adee99c60cb9a2a7cb87600b5f.png)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301831 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301946 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1069 "q-327144" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1072 #uuid "8ff48842-7624-55b0-a144-af0fbc008e11" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302327 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40(0)+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25\\,[{y}^{2}+4y+4] & =0 \\\\ 25\\,(y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n![](images/78/78be4ca7215ee8dbf05a442080bae752e4fadfbe76084d975338d00e1bb02c78.png)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301534 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301909 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1069 "q-327146" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1072 #uuid "8ea97f79-2635-519c-82bb-e437d3073998" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302328 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301255 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301607 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1069 "q-327148" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1072 #uuid "e0e6d437-c042-571f-a8a4-bf2beee49c2b" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302329 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-12(0)+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,).$\n\n![](images/94/94a5bd2df6ae6177e62b66a0a978a358d73fed255e425337812887c81b9d2d10.png)" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301297 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1069 "q-327160" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$*" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1072 #uuid "c7343f41-95b5-5176-9f70-e4d55b426d92" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302330 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2(0)} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=-3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301536 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302017 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1069 "q-327163" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $5{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-20x+16y+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1072 #uuid "ab0f43f6-7870-5ec6-af4a-e401ee4f1c5e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302331 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-20x+16y+20 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4\\cdot {0}^{2}-20x+16\\cdot 0+20 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-20x+20 & =0 \\\\ 5\\,[{x}^{2}-4x]+20 & =0 \\\\ 5[(x-2)^{2}-4]+20 & =0 \\\\ 5(x-2)^{2}-20+20 & =0 \\\\ 5(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(2,0).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301775 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301862 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302332 1069 "q-327171" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302332 1071 "![](images/61/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametr