's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}) & =7\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301923 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1069 "q-327771" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1071 "Find the exact value of $4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{15}^{∘}.$\n\n$4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{15}^{∘}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1072 #uuid "7de355f4-68ed-5d8f-8bd0-5f9d51794ae9" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302353 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={15}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({15}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({15}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({15}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301761 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301953 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1069 "q-327780" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$  for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1072 #uuid "715f065a-25f9-5a41-9131-051c595dcf7c" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302354 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)=(75.963\\,7…)^{∘}≈{75.964}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${75.964}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is ${x}_{1}={76}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}+{75.964}^{∘}≈{256}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={76}^{∘}$ and $x={256}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301730 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301881 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1069 "q-327790" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{5}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}} and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1072 #uuid "760a1f94-2e63-5945-8d27-6f6515775dee" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302355 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})=0.674\\,741…≈0.674\\,7\n$$\n\nSince $0.674\\,7$ radians lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is ${x}_{1}=0.7.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi +0.674\\,7≈3.8,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=3.8.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301958 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1069 "q-327798" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1072 #uuid "bcd20c00-207d-5776-ad99-e9ea0fea55dc" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302356 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301302 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1069 "q-327803" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1072 #uuid "cf7a561b-81b3-5465-9ed7-22d55b60ba48" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302357 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301743 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1069 "q-327808" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1072 #uuid "42002e7f-0b06-57fa-9bb9-6d321bc37cf7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302358 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301362 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1069 "q-327809" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1072 #uuid "8c43bad9-0cfd-52b5-8797-68675d788468" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302359 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301375 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1069 "q-327811" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1072 #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302360 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [175921