db-56bf-8b0f-4abc2119254d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302365 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/10/101d9b26027bc5667009b0c7af69fa190c987c362122402fce62daec5833092f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301550 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301920 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1069 "q-327847" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1071 "![](images/ab/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1072 #uuid "c61885c3-83e2-5a8d-b365-c4d1e934aa7d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302366 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](images/df/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301484 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1069 "q-32785" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t-3 \\\\ y=7-9t \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1072 #uuid "9af4fe7c-30e2-5561-acae-f8071602310e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302367 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3t-3 \\\\ 7-9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ -9t \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-3,7,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301540 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301618 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301929 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302034 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1069 "q-327851" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{4t}+1,\\,y=\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1072 #uuid "48a3ec09-02a4-56d8-97ff-afaf8c99de7a" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302368 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{4t}+1\\ge 1$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{t}+2\\ge 2$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301475 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301769 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301901 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1069 "q-327852" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{8-t},\\,y=\\sqrt{16-2t}+1,\\,t<8,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1072 #uuid "1f6727c7-1507-583d-a603-85c34e4d8ab3" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302369 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{8-t}>0$ for all $t<8.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{16-2t}+1>1$ for all $t<8.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny>1.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301367 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301449 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301489 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301515 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301521 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301577 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1069 "q-327861" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}} $\\le $\n$x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1072 #uuid "b4154359-18f0-56b5-8f88-83b3d5f9fa49" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302370 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ }\\ge -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -3 \\\\ -3 & \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301395 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301656 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301698 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301836 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301856 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301999 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1069 "q-327866" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}{{field-5}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-6}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1072 #uuid "400ebb98-16e3-549a-88aa-0bf9b1800aca" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302371 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301679 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1069 "q-32787" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1071 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(1,-\\sqrt{2},-3)$ and parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1072 #uuid "e10c797f-5ff9-53d5-8918-b678cb0a3bad" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302372 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301324 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301342 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301622 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301666 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301705 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301939 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1069 "q-327871" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1072 #uuid "d4e18147-144f-5a8b-8dfa-04f1a9a6fd21" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302373 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}+2 & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le \\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{5}{4}\\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301409 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1069 "q-327876" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{60}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=b+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={195}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $b$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1072 #uuid "61472c10-65bc-581e-a654-9e21777d2b7f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302374 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{60}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 2x-{60}^{∘} & ={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={210}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={330}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 2x & ={270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={390}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={135}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={195}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b={135}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301506 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301670 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301718 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301876 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301991 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302010 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1069 "q-327880" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1072 #uuid "dc80a819-3c24-5625-baac-562a70097b16" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302375 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4+1 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 4+1 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{7}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301253 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301445 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301820 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301825 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301830 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302001 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1069 "q-327884" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1072 #uuid "519df9e7-1dc6-5ad7-93ad-7804c67f997d" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302376 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\