33413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302395 1071 "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302395 1072 #uuid "bde96fe6-6e2d-5275-8a67-e4ee0d456370" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302395 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=-{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301510 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301758 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1069 "q-328001" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1071 "Express $-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1072 #uuid "dddb6c53-8f38-5969-8eb0-770120aca4ed" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302396 1074 "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1069 "q-328007" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1071 "Given that the expression $-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1072 #uuid "f3e045d2-6cd9-5743-8c88-8e195ab07316" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302397 1074 "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301628 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1069 "q-32804" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1071 "Given $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i-2t\\,j+(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k$, find $({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1072 #uuid "19486880-810b-5bd6-a22c-f282191bb018" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302398 1074 "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+5\\cdot 2t\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10t)\\,k \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2t\\,i+(0)j+(10)\\,k \\\\  & =12t\\,i+10\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(12t\\,i+10\\,k)\\cdot (2{t}^{3}\\,i-2t\\,j+(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k)+(6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k) \\\\  & =(12t\\cdot 2{t}^{3}+0\\cdot (-2t)+10\\cdot (1+5{t}^{2}))+((6{t}^{2})^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(10t)^{2}) \\\\  & =24{t}^{4}+0+10+50{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+4+100{t}^{2} \\\\  & =60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301457 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1069 "q-328066" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1071 "![](images/0e/0eccac1629f5ab7d80fe4a7a6d4b1f58867adeed023e957f1f52daa885a745fa.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{UT}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{RQ}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=$ \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1072 #uuid "0d0c63da-6a67-5920-99e3-8972f577bd75" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302399 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $R$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=\\overset{\\to }{RP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3abc51a0c48faa7853eb2ef605412ca6311866994b15a579b7bc7e7db63ee15.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RP}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/a5/a53de4a8dcbd4a5d1f7640d5e1d4c8b6fd6e5e24388f432d279392727b11dd59.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{RP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =-d+(-a) \\\\  & =-d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301848 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1069 "q-328086" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1071 "![](images/67/6709c80eeddea3d31d68d51816a0249758d5a535eb5f2e6983f8e786faf3f227.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QT}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{QT}=$ \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1072 #uuid "87795686-9201-59f8-84b6-412f31acf827" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302400 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{ST},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8dc0d2e01927c8b2ca116265b257d835a1baae62272b703610f2947d92a8c2.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=-b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{ST}=-c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed8c71e0d45ae0c9cf7d8a7d2889ee3a79471aaeb408ad0f53f487523daac13.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{ST} \\\\  & =-b+(-c) \\\\  & =-b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302021 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1069 "q-328087" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1071 "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1072 #uuid "4fabc70d-25ca-5a30-9a52-90a915524efa" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302401 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{24})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301426 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1069 "q-328099" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1071 "![](images/40/40fa465ab7fc211d2e4a9bb8640e12d3bb2d694eae834f514c66e53dc11fd96d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{ST}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TQ}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=d,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TR}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{SP}$ in terms of vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{SP}=$  \n {{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1072 #uuid "6f56c966-724d-56f4-9c43-df79e0be0f12" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302402 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $S$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SP}=\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/07/07aebf5a6aaa98c9ec5d4e9801dbe7d2cf3ade86573bcd8ee56db375f3e38575.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ST}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TQ}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8c193472acbe72979f8a78fe9bd02726ad98c166381434175d0586bb574cb6.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SP} & =\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =a+b+d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301408 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1069 "q-32817" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1071 "![](images/1f/1ffc0945ea74b1bd467a0b9935ee28b88be004c18b11492d67ecdfec56e1bb09.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1072 #uuid "00c48028-299a-590c-8308-e04090d60774" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302403 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,2)$ and $Q(2,4,5).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+3t \\\\ 3+t \\\\ 2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+3t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301850 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1069 "q-32818" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1071 "![](images/99/99504eaa41497c5be9baa7c860c97ab5ba5c858609e47ad347f24ec9b8ffc940.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1072 #uuid "add06203-1905-5fcf-9c44-6a4bc76cf55f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302404 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(3,6,8)$ and $Q(-2,-5,4).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5t \\\\ -11t \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-5t \\\\ 6-11t \\\\ 8-4t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5t \\\\ y=6-11t \\\\ z=8-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301318 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301634 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301637 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301703 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1069 "q-328216" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1071 "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1072 #uuid "fdbedcf9-1ae3-563d-af96-ceded480cb29" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302405 1074 "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=2v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=2.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301606 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301789 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301826 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302406 1068 #edb/ref 17