ollowing trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{125}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=-{40}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302411 1072 #uuid "3dcfb529-682e-501c-b64b-22ac2ba40cda" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302411 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{125}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{125}^{∘} & ={315}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={45}^{∘}-{125}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={315}^{∘}-{125}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 2x & =-{80}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={190}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & =-{40}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={95}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={95}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301650 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1069 "q-328240" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{50}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=a+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={175}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $({0}^{∘},{90}^{∘}].$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1072 #uuid "cb1d52b8-1d5c-51f9-9487-45f8fa427c62" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302412 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{50}^{∘} & ={135}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & x+{50}^{∘} & ={225}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={135}^{∘}-{50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={225}^{∘}-{50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={85}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={175}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={85}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301465 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1069 "q-328241" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx={720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1072 #uuid "ee30a758-7016-5793-a211-943189d101ed" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302413 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={150}^{∘}-{150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={210}^{∘}-{150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & ={360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={720}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={120}^{∘}+{720}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={120}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301242 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1069 "q-328248" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{6}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1072 #uuid "5731fad9-ebde-5b11-9201-0bc52d077579" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302414 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{12}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301394 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1069 "q-328250" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{30}^{∘})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+n\\cdot {90}^{∘},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1072 #uuid "21b7e6e8-3683-538e-8568-18f7a221cf41" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302415 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{30}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={37.5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=37.5.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301682 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301787 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1069 "q-328251" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1072 #uuid "0f026992-c374-503e-a4fe-dd6cee19903f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302416 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-5$ and $k=3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-5,3\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301887 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302009 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1069 "q-328263" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-13)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{64}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1072 #uuid "6299deab-2629-5fdd-830f-03125860d622" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302417 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-13)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{64}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=13$ and $k=6.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,13,6\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301829 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301987 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1069 "q-328264" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(y+11)^{2}}{49}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1072 #uuid "62bdfc70-e5ac-538a-a5c1-ca677e8fe069" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302418 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-6))^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(y-(-11))^{2}}{49}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-6$ and $k=-11.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-6,-11).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301717 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1069 "q-32827" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(0,-1,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1072 #uuid "3f334edf-1682-5eef-8043-6ee3979dea32" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302419 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301267 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301372 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1069 "q-328280" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1072 #uuid "cb7809a7-4f27-5a73-bb84-199c1f557824" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302420 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{5}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=4$ and $k=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,4,-3\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301821 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1069 "q-32829" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1071 "![](images/58/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1072 #uuid "7a883673-7d08-54f5-83e2-ff286d2a402e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302421 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301364 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301978 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1069 "q-328291" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(x+11)^{2}}{49}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1072 #uuid "ef1a9396-3c80-51c0-9e43-3edcd50df270" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302422 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-11))^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-11$ and $k=7.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-11,7\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301413 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301763 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1069 "q-328295" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+12)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{121}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1072 #uuid "7de4b2f4-5e99-52eb-8be5-60106f38cec0" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302423 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-12))^{2}}{{7}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{{11}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-5$ and $k=-12.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-5,-12\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301529 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301788 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1069 "q-328296" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{49}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1072 #uuid "bf1b960b-5742-5bae-bebe-61fa7f6ecf8f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302424 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{{8}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=3$ and $k=6.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,6\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301480 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301863 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1069 "q-328298" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-9)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(x-14)^{2}}{36}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1072 #uuid "21eb74f9-7619-592c-9a89-e44136de46b7" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302425 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-9)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-14)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=14$ and $k=9.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,14,9\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301593 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1069 "q-328306" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1071 "| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | $\\,0.9484\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n\nApproximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.9480$ using interval bisection and the above table.\n\n$z≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1072 #uuid "f974be1b-50fc-5e85-a97a-26d0236743e8" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302426 1074 "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.9480$ lies between. These numbers are $0.9474$ and $0.9484.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.9474 & <0.9480<0.9484\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $Φ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.62) & <Φ(z)<Φ(1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz≈\\frac{1.62+1.63}{2}=1.625.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301700 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301959 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1069 "q-328312" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1072 #uuid "042a400e-bc31-5bbb-9a40-e7f01965d1ce" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302427 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-8))^{2}}{{10}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=0$ and $k=-8.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,0,-8\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301582 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1069 "q-328313" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1071 "| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ |\n\nApproximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.3725$ using interval bisection and the above table\n\n$z≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1072 #uuid "2f15f383-3ed4-5313-890e-c4935cc7b090" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302428 1074 "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.3725$ lies between. These numbers are $0.3707$ and $0.3745.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3707 & <0.3725<0.3745\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $Φ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.33) & <Φ(z)<Φ(-0.32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz≈\\frac{-0.33+(-0.32)}{2}=-0.325.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301667 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301967 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1069 "q-328317" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{81}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1072 #uuid "a38cb36d-c0e0-5001-b269-6ab08e9aaaff" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302429 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=3$ and $k=0.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,0\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301693 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301951 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1069 "q-328327" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1071 "![](images/52/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1072 #uuid "2e29b9a7-ba3d-59be-8f85-186fc6a70498" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302430 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](images/1b/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301711 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301989 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1069 "q-328331" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1072 #uuid "f49c8b56-8b4b-5d42-9a44-609ea5df6909" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302431 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{30}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={60}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={210}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={210}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301681 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1069 "q-32835" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1071 "Given $f(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,2t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩$, find $({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1072 #uuid "38bbc30d-2715-5fd5-8cc3-adae49e8b272" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302432 1074 "Using the dot product rule, we can write: \n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,6t⟩\\cdot ⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,2t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩+⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot (2t)+6t\\cdot ({t}^{3}-1))+((2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-2+0+6{t}^{4}-6t+4{t}^{2}+4+9{t}^{4} \\\\  & =15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301406 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301509 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301609 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301766 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1069 "q-328350" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $3tan\\,(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1072 #uuid "f8798d95-6336-5f26-817e-c5e7e84cb46f" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302433 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301317 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301567 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301689 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302020 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1069 "q-328351" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘})+4=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1072 #uuid "5af2f4f9-02e7-5944-adb2-89b602b3730e" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302434 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘})+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘}) & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{45}^{∘} & =-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={45}^{∘}-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={0}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={180}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={270}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={270}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301961 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302030 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1069 "q-328353" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $3{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-12x+16y=28$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1072 #uuid "09c925d6-50d3-5bb2-a85f-2c23d9456fed" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302435 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-12x+16y & =28 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-12x-4{y}^{2}+16y & =28 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-4x]-4[{y}^{2}-4y] & =28 \\\\ 3[(x-2)^{2}-4]-4[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =28 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2}-12-4(y-2)^{2}+16 & =28 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2}-4(y-2)^{2} & =28+12-16 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2}-4(y-2)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,2,2\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301269 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301627 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1069 "q-328355" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-8x+6y=11$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1072 #uuid "d8162c3a-d644-506a-968b-c4b5bfa37969" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302436 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-8x+6y & =11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x)-({y}^{2}-6y) & =11 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1]-[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =11 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4-(y-3)^{2}+9 & =11 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-(y-3)^{2} & =11+4-9 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-(y-3)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3/2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,1,3\\,).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301264 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301438 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1069 "q-328358" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-8x+2y=7$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533413 17592186045585 true] [17592186302437 1072 #u